  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nAs of 1 AM CDT, temperatures across the region are in the upper 30s \nto low 40s with winds out of the northeast to southeast at generally \nless than 10 mph. There is potential for fog this morning across \nnorth central SD and the eastern side of the Sisseton Hills but \nwinds are a bit high and there isn't much moisture sitting around. \nIf fog does form, it should burn off by mid morning. The rest of the \nday is expected to be cloudy, except for some afternoon clearing \nnorth of Hwy 12 and east of US 281. Highs today will be in the 50s.\n\nThe main story for the forecast will be our precipitation chances. \nThe first will be Friday night into Saturday and the second, more \nwidespread and wet event starting Monday. A low pressure system will \nsit just off the lee side of the Rockies Friday with a front moving \nup from the south and kicking off some light showers through the day \nSaturday. Total accumulations of a couple hundredths are expected. A \nlow pressure system will move in from the west Monday, exiting the \nregion Tuesday. We could continue to see some wrap around moisture \nthrough Wednesday morning. PWAT values Monday are generally between \n1 and 1.25 inches across the forecast area, in the 99th or above \nNAEFS percentile. These PWAT values start to decrease towards 0.5 \ninches through the day Tuesday. The potential for more than an inch \nof precipitation from Monday morning through Wednesday morning is 50 \nto 75% along and east of the Missouri River. The chance for more \nthan 2 inches during the same time frame is 40 to 50% in the \nnorthern portion of the James Valley and along the I-29 corridor. \n\nTemperatures for the first half of the forecast will be 10 to 15 \ndegrees above average before colder air moves in behind the low and \ndrops temps down to around average. Winds the next few days will \ngust around 25 mph during the afternoon hours. Monday, with the \nincoming low, winds will increase to 35-40 mph across most of the \nforecast area, perhaps reaching 45 mph or higher across central SD. \nFog will also be possible after the rain Saturday, however, cloud \ncover may keep this from materializing.",
